.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt ZFS-PRUNE-SNAPSHOTS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-prune-snapshots
.Nd destroy all but the newest snapshots of datasets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dnrv
.Op Fl k Ar number
.Ar dataset ...
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps the newest
.Ar number
snapshots of each dataset named, by creation time, and destroys every
older one.
The snapshots of a dataset are destroyed in one
.Ql zfs destroy dataset@%snapshot
range, from the oldest through the newest that goes, so a prune of
hundreds of snapshots is one command, with one failure to report.
A dataset with no more than
.Ar number
snapshots is left alone.
.Pp
Without
.Fl r ,
only the snapshots of the dataset itself are counted.
With
.Fl r ,
each of its descendants is pruned as a dataset of its own, and keeps
its own
.Ar number .
A dataset that fails does not stop the others.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-k number"
.It Fl d
Ask for deferred destruction
.Pq Ql zfs destroy -d ,
so that a snapshot which cannot be destroyed at once, being held or
cloned, is destroyed when it can be.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl k Ar number
The newest snapshots to keep, a whole number; the default is 1, and 0
destroys them all.
.It Fl n
A dry run:
.Xr zfs 8
is asked what it would destroy
.Pq Ql zfs destroy -n -v ,
and says so on stdout; nothing is destroyed.
.It Fl r
Also prune the descendants of each dataset.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl v
Report each dataset pruned, with how many snapshots were destroyed, up
to which one, on stderr.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev PATH
.Pa /sbin
and
.Pa /usr/sbin
are added at its end, where
.Xr zfs 8
lives, since a Linux cron job's
.Ev PATH
and an unprivileged Linux user's leave them out.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Every dataset was pruned.
.It 1
The snapshots of some dataset could not be listed or destroyed; it is
named on stderr.
.It 2
A usage error, a
.Fl k
that is not a whole number among them.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Keep the three newest snapshots of a dataset and of every one under it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs-prune-snapshots -r -k 3 tank/backup
.Ed
.Pp
What it would destroy, without destroying it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs-prune-snapshots -n -v -k 3 tank/backup
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs-snapshot 1 ,
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-destroy 8
